## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a beginner-friendly iOS app with real-world constraints: offline data via Core Data, clean UI with SwiftUI, and responsive search using Combine. It also probes data modeling, basic UI composition, and practical testing considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Core Data offline cache
- SwiftUI List with custom badge visuals
- Combine debounced search for responsive UX
- Simple data modeling for Delivery

## Code Example
```swift
class Delivery: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged var id: String
  @NSManaged var address: String
  @NSManaged var eta: Date
  @NSManaged var status: String
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the debounced search behavior?
- How would you handle sync when the network becomes available again?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic SwiftUI state management, on-device persistence, live UI updates, and accessibility considerations in a beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI @AppStorage for lightweight persistence
- Color theming and dynamic UI updates
- Accessibility labeling and dynamic type considerations
- Basic test planning: persistence, accessibility, UI consistency

## Code Example
```swift
@AppStorage("themeColor") private var themeColorHex: String = "#4285F4"
@AppStorage("fontSize") private var fontSize: Double = 14.0

var body: some View {
  VStack {
    // color palette and font size slider UI here
    Text("Preview text").font(.system(size: CGFloat(fontSize)))
      .foregroundColor(Color(hex: themeColorHex))
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate edge cases like invalid saved color values?
- How would you extend this to support dark mode vs light mode themes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a simple, offline-first persistence layer with Codable, basic CRUD in a SwiftUI app, and derive a user-facing metric (streak) from daily entries. It avoids external dependencies and targets beginner-friendly data modeling and UI work.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI, Codable, FileManager and Documents directory usage
- Data model design for Habit and DayEntry
- Derived metrics: computing daily streaks from consecutive entries
- Basic CRUD operations and crash-safe resume through persistent storage
- Lightweight test plan focusing on persistence and streak logic

## Code Example
```swift
struct DayEntry: Codable { let date: Date; let completed: Bool }
struct Habit: Codable { let id: UUID; var name: String; var colorHex: String; var entries: [DayEntry] }

let url = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!.appendingPathComponent("habits.json")
func save(_ habits: [Habit]) throws {
  let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(habits)
  try data.write(to: url)
}
func load() -> [Habit] {
  guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: url) else { return [] }
  return (try? JSONDecoder().decode([Habit].self, from: data)) ?? []
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle time zones and daylight saving when computing streaks?
- How would you extend this to sync across devices, while preserving offline-first behavior?

